---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import CodeblockSample from "../../components/CodeblockSample.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import PluginReference from "../../components/PluginReference.astro";
import { getPlugin } from "../../data/plugins";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

const plugin = getPlugin("codeblock")!;

const tocEntries = [
  { id: "what-it-does", text: "What it does", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "install", text: "Install", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "register", text: "Register", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "configuration", text: "Configuration", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "add-the-component", text: "Add the component", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "live-demo", text: "Live demo", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "examples", text: "Examples", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "props", text: "Props", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "css-tokens", text: "CSS custom properties", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "edge-cases", text: "Edge cases", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "for-agents", text: "For agents", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
];
---

<Doc
  tocEntries={tocEntries}
  currentSlug="codeblock"
  title="codeblock"
  section="@plugdash/codeblock"
  description="Shiki syntax highlighting for EmDash code blocks, done when the post is saved."
>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    Highlights the <code>code</code> blocks EmDash already stores with
    Shiki. On save, a <code>content:beforeSave</code> hook runs each block
    through Shiki and stores the HTML on the block
    (<code>pdHighlight</code>), so a page view prints stored HTML and never
    loads Shiki. The only client JavaScript is a small click listener for
    the copy button.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stored HTML carries a key built from the code, language, themes,
    line numbers setting and plugin version. Change any of them and the
    block is highlighted at render time until the post is saved again. So
    after a theme change old posts still render correctly, and re-saving
    makes them fast again. Highlighting never blocks a save: if Shiki fails
    or takes over 30 seconds, the post saves without stored HTML.
  </p>
  <p>
    codeblock is a Native plugin - it needs Shiki, which is a Node
    dependency, so it can't be sandboxed or published to the marketplace.
    It registers no new editor block type; it renders the <code>code</code>
    block EmDash already has. It declares <code>content:write</code>,
    which the save hook needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <InstallCommand command={plugin.installCommand} />

  <h2 id="register">Register</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>astro.config.mjs</code> at the root of your Astro project.
    Import the plugin at the top, then add it to the <code>plugins</code>
    array of the <code>emdash()</code> integration - the one that sits
    inside Astro's top-level <code>integrations</code> array:
  </p>
  <Code language="js" filename="astro.config.mjs" code={`import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
import emdash from "emdash/astro"
import { codeblockPlugin } from "@plugdash/codeblock"

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    emdash({
      // native - must be in plugins, not sandboxed
      plugins: [
        codeblockPlugin({ theme: "github-dark", lineNumbers: true }),
      ],
    }),
  ],
})`} />

  <h2 id="configuration">Configuration</h2>
  <p>All options are optional. Defaults cover most sites out of the box.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>theme</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>github-dark</code></td><td>Shiki theme name. Unknown names fall back to the default.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>lightTheme</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>github-light</code> when no theme is set</td><td>Second theme for light mode, emitted as CSS variables. Setting <code>theme</code> alone turns light mode off.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>langs</code></td><td><code>string[]</code></td><td>common set</td><td>Languages loaded up front. Anything else loads on first use.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>lineNumbers</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Render line numbers in the gutter.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Preloaded by default: TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Rust, Shell,
    JSON, YAML, Markdown, HTML, CSS, SQL.
  </p>

  <h2 id="add-the-component">Add the component</h2>
  <p>
    The component is auto-wired into <code>&lt;PortableText&gt;</code>
    through <code>componentsEntry</code> - no manual block mapping needed.
    Once the plugin is registered, existing <code>code</code> blocks start
    rendering highlighted, at render time until each post is next saved.
  </p>
  <p>For direct usage outside Portable Text, import the component:</p>
  <Code language="astro" code={`---
import CodeBlock from "@plugdash/codeblock/CodeBlock.astro"
---
<CodeBlock code={source} language="python" filename="app.py" lineNumbers />`} />

  <h2 id="live-demo">Live demo</h2>
  <p>
    Rendered by the real <code>CodeBlock.astro</code> on this page. The last
    one uses a language Shiki doesn't know, so it falls back to plaintext.
  </p>
  <CodeblockSample />

  <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>
  <p>
    A site that wants light and dark themes to switch with the reader's
    system preference passes both:
  </p>
  <Code language="js" code={`codeblockPlugin({
  theme: "github-dark",
  lightTheme: "github-light",
})`} />
  <p>
    In the editor, an author picks a language on the code block and
    writes the source. EmDash keeps it as a <code>code</code> block in the
    Portable Text body, and on save codeblock adds the highlighted HTML
    next to it:
  </p>
  <Code language="json" code={`{
  "_type": "code",
  "code": "def greet(name):\\n    return f\\"hi {name}\\"",
  "language": "python",
  "filename": "greet.py",
  "pdHighlight": { "key": "...", "html": "<pre class=\\"shiki github-dark\\">..." }
}`} />
  <p>
    On the site, that block renders as a header bar with the filename
    and language, followed by Shiki's highlighted markup - a
    <code>&lt;pre class="shiki github-dark"&gt;</code> wrapper with a
    coloured <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> per token. If <code>lineNumbers</code>
    is on, a numbered gutter runs down the left edge, built from a CSS
    counter so the numbers never end up in a reader's copy-paste.
  </p>

  <PluginReference plugin={plugin} />

  <h2 id="edge-cases">Edge cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Unknown or missing language renders as plaintext instead of throwing.</li>
    <li><code>text</code>, <code>txt</code>, <code>plain</code>, <code>plaintext</code>, and <code>none</code> all mean plaintext.</li>
    <li>Empty code renders an empty <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> block.</li>
    <li>Code over 10,000 lines is cut off with a trailing truncation notice.</li>
    <li>Unknown theme names fall back to <code>github-dark</code>.</li>
    <li>Blocks whose HTML is over 100,000 characters are not stored; they are highlighted at render time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No line highlighting or diff view. No client-side highlighting, so no
    runtime theme or language switching: pick a theme (or a light/dark
    pair) at registration time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="for-agents">For agents</h2>
  <p>
    After installing and registering the plugin, no further setup is
    needed for rendering - block components are auto-wired into
    <code>&lt;PortableText&gt;</code> and existing <code>code</code>
    blocks start rendering highlighted immediately. To verify, save a
    post containing a code block with a language set, then view the
    page source: it should contain <code>class="shiki github-dark"</code>
    (or whatever theme was configured) with coloured <code>style</code>
    attributes on the token spans. If nothing renders differently,
    confirm the plugin is in the <code>plugins</code> array, not
    <code>sandboxed</code> - native plugins cannot run sandboxed.
  </p>
</Doc>
